<section class="section" id="life">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px;">
    <span class="eyebrow">Life</span>
    <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.6rem,1.2rem+1.6vw,2.2rem);">From Patna to Queen Elizabeth House</h2>
    <figure class="bookcover bookcover--left">
      <img src="{{ "/assets/img/sanjaya-portrait-close.jpg" | url }}" alt="Portrait photograph of Sanjaya Lall" width="404" height="361" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>Sanjaya Lall</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Sanjaya Lall was born in Patna, in the Indian state of Bihar, on 13 December 1940,
      a grandson of the historian K.&nbsp;P. Jayaswal. Academic distinction came early and
      never left: a gold medal for ranking first in his BA at Patna University in 1960; a
      first in Philosophy, Politics and Economics at St John's College, Oxford, in 1963,
      with the college prize; a distinction in the Oxford MPhil in economics in 1965.
    </p>
    <p>
      He went straight from the MPhil to the World Bank as an economist, returning to
      Oxford in 1968. Apart from a second Washington stint in the 1980s,
      <span class="hl">Oxford remained his home for the rest of his life</span>: research officer
      at the Institute of Economics and Statistics, University Lecturer in Development
      Economics at Queen Elizabeth House, Fellow of Green College from 1982, course
      director of Development Studies, a founding editor of <em>Oxford Development
      Studies</em>, and from 1999 Professor of Development Economics. For Lall,
      development economics was never primarily the study of poverty: it was the study of
      how poor countries build the industrial, technical, and organisational capabilities
      to compete &mdash; and everything he did followed from that definition of the
      problem.
    </p>
    <div class="photopair" style="--photo-h:225px;">
      <figure>
        <img src="{{ "/assets/img/sanjaya-rani-walk.jpg" | url }}" alt="Sanjaya Lall and his wife Rani on a path across parkland in early spring" width="760" height="525" loading="lazy">
        <figcaption>With Rani, March 1999</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src="{{ "/assets/img/sanjaya-family-ruins.jpg" | url }}" alt="Sanjaya Lall with his family beside ancient stone ruins" width="760" height="529" loading="lazy">
        <figcaption>With his family, on holiday</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <p>
      The output was prodigious &mdash; more than thirty books, some seventy-five refereed
      journal articles, over seventy book chapters, and dozens of reports for governments
      and international agencies. Few economists of his generation were as deeply engaged
      in practice: he advised the World Bank, the OECD, the ILO, UNICEF, the European
      Commission, and the Commonwealth Secretariat, and served as <span class="hl">principal
      consultant to UNCTAD's <em>World Investment Report</em> and to UNIDO's <em>Industrial
      Development Report</em></span>. Yet he refused every offer to join these organisations
      permanently, insisting on the independence to tell them what the evidence said
      rather than what they wished to hear &mdash; it was the influence the work might
      carry, he would say, not the organisation, that mattered. Two intellectual debts
      shaped the work: Paul Streeten, his early mentor and collaborator at Oxford, and
      &mdash; at a distance &mdash; Nicholas Kaldor, whose conviction that manufacturing
      is the engine of growth runs through everything Lall wrote.
    </p>
    <div class="photopair" style="--photo-h:265px;">
      <figure>
        <img src="{{ "/assets/img/sanjaya-rani-garden.jpg" | url }}" alt="Sanjaya Lall standing beside his wife Rani, seated in a garden" width="620" height="849" loading="lazy">
        <figcaption>With his wife, Rani, in their garden</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src="{{ "/assets/img/sanjaya-dinner.jpg" | url }}" alt="Sanjaya Lall seated at a dinner table" width="560" height="804" loading="lazy">
        <figcaption>At dinner</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--mist" id="multinationals">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px;">
    <span class="eyebrow">First contribution</span>
    <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.6rem,1.2rem+1.6vw,2.2rem);">Multinationals, seen from the South</h2>
    <p>
      Lall's first mark on the discipline came in the early 1970s, with pioneering
      empirical studies of <span class="hl">transfer pricing by multinational firms</span>. Working with
      evidence from the pharmaceutical industry, he showed how corporations could use the
      prices charged on transactions between their own subsidiaries to move profits
      quietly out of developing host countries &mdash; a practice largely invisible to the
      tax authorities of the day. Half a century later, when the taxation of multinational
      profit-shifting commands G20 summits, the work reads as remarkably far ahead of its
      time.
    </p>
    <figure class="bookcover">
      <img src="{{ "/assets/img/books/foreign-investment-transnationals.jpg" | url }}" alt="Cover of Foreign Investment, Transnationals and Developing Countries" width="360" height="556" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>With Paul Streeten &middot; Macmillan, 1977</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The transfer-pricing findings fed a larger project, pursued in part with Streeten:
      an unsentimental accounting of what foreign direct investment actually does for
      development. <em>Foreign Investment, Transnationals and Developing Countries</em>
      (1977) took a deliberately evidence-first path between the two camps of its era. At
      a time when much of development economics saw multinationals only as instruments of
      dependency, and much of mainstream economics saw them only as carriers of capital
      and know-how, Lall and Streeten showed that <span class="hl--coral">the benefits were real but
      conditional</span> &mdash; on the host country's bargaining position, its policies, and
      above all its capacity to absorb what the foreign firm brought. Much of Lall's later
      work sharpened the point into operational questions: under what circumstances do
      foreign affiliates form productive linkages with local firms, and how can host
      economies avoid having their own enterprise crowded out?
    </p>
    <p>
      The essays collected in <em>The Multinational Corporation</em> (1980),
      <em>Developing Countries in the International Economy</em> (1981), and
      <em>Multinationals, Technology and Exports</em> (1985) extended the account. And
      from his abiding fascination with India came a genuinely new field: with
      <em>Developing Countries as Exporters of Technology</em> (1982) and <em>The New
      Multinationals</em> (1983), Lall was among the first economists to study
      <span class="hl">Third World multinationals</span> &mdash; developing-country firms investing
      abroad and selling technology. When he began, the Fortune&nbsp;500 contained barely
      a handful of developing-country firms; it now contains scores. He was writing
      decades before the rise of the great emerging-market corporations made the
      phenomenon impossible to ignore.
    </p>
    <div class="bookshelf">
      <figure class="bookcover">
        <img src="{{ "/assets/img/books/the-multinational-corporation.jpg" | url }}" alt="Cover of The Multinational Corporation" width="153" height="236" loading="lazy">
        <figcaption>Macmillan, 1980</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="bookcover">
        <img src="{{ "/assets/img/books/developing-countries-international-economy.jpg" | url }}" alt="Cover of Developing Countries in the International Economy" width="153" height="241" loading="lazy">
        <figcaption>Macmillan, 1981</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="bookcover">
        <img src="{{ "/assets/img/books/multinationals-technology-exports.jpg" | url }}" alt="Cover of Multinationals, Technology and Exports" width="153" height="236" loading="lazy">
        <figcaption>Macmillan, 1985</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="bookcover">
        <img src="{{ "/assets/img/books/theory-and-reality-in-development.jpg" | url }}" alt="Cover of Theory and Reality in Development" width="360" height="593" loading="lazy">
        <figcaption>Ed. with Frances Stewart &middot; Macmillan, 1986</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <p>
      The debt to Streeten was repaid in kind: with Frances Stewart, Lall edited
      <em>Theory and Reality in Development</em> (1986), a volume of essays in his
      mentor's honour &mdash; a form of tribute that, in time, would be paid to Lall
      himself.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="capability">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px;">
    <span class="eyebrow">Second contribution</span>
    <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.6rem,1.2rem+1.6vw,2.2rem);">How countries learn: technological capability</h2>
    <figure class="bookcover bookcover--left">
      <img src="{{ "/assets/img/books/learning-to-industrialize.jpg" | url }}" alt="Cover of Learning to Industrialize" width="166" height="256" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>Macmillan, 1987</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The idea for which Lall is best remembered grew out of a dissatisfaction. Economic
      theory treated technology as a black box &mdash; a blueprint that, once purchased or
      imported, worked anywhere. Lall's fieldwork told him otherwise. Mastering a
      technology, he argued, is itself a costly, risky, skill-intensive process:
      <span class="hl">firms do not simply receive technology; they must learn it</span>, adapt it,
      and build the engineering, organisational, and managerial competence to use and
      improve it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Learning to Industrialize</em> (1987) traced that acquisition of capability
      through detailed studies of Indian firms. The framework was generalised in
      "Technological Capabilities and Industrialization" (<em>World Development</em>,
      1992), which remains his most cited paper and a foundational text of the field. It
      distinguished the capabilities needed to invest, to produce, and to link with other
      firms; showed how they interact with a country's incentives, factor markets, and
      institutions; and explained why <span class="hl--coral">national technological development is
      cumulative and path-dependent</span> &mdash; why, in short, countries that look similar
      on paper industrialise so differently in practice.
    </p>
    <p>
      The approach placed Lall in a distinguished lineage running from Marshall through
      Rosenstein-Rodan and Scitovsky &mdash; thinkers who refused to reduce technical
      change to a term in a production function. He was among the earliest to reject the
      then-standard practice of estimating technology spillovers through aggregate
      production functions, insisting that learning had to be observed where it happens,
      inside firms, as they fight their way from competing on price to competing on
      products. And it gave development policy a new object: if capability is built
      rather than bestowed, then the interesting question is what builds it.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--mist" id="industrial-policy">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px;">
    <span class="eyebrow">Third contribution</span>
    <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.6rem,1.2rem+1.6vw,2.2rem);">Creating winners: the industrial policy debate</h2>
    <figure class="bookcover">
      <img src="{{ "/assets/img/books/learning-from-the-asian-tigers.jpg" | url }}" alt="Cover of Learning from the Asian Tigers" width="360" height="556" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>Macmillan, 1996</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The answer, Lall argued, was visible in East Asia &mdash; if one was willing to look
      at what its governments had actually done. Through the 1990s, as the Washington
      consensus hardened around the view that openness alone would deliver technology and
      growth, Lall assembled the counter-evidence. His 1994 critique of the World Bank's
      <em>East Asian Miracle</em> report, and the studies gathered in <em>Learning from
      the Asian Tigers</em> (1996), made the case that Korea, Taiwan, and their neighbours
      had not merely "picked" industrial winners: they had <span class="hl">deliberately
      created them</span>, using selective, disciplined intervention to overcome the market
      failures that beset costly, uncertain technological learning.
    </p>
    <p>
      This was not a licence for protectionism, and Lall was scathing about
      import-substitution regimes that sheltered firms from any pressure to learn. The
      East Asian lesson, in his telling, was the pairing of support with
      <span class="hl--coral">export discipline</span> &mdash; help that had to be earned in world
      markets &mdash; and of intervention with the steady construction of skills,
      infrastructure, and institutions. He called such measures "market-stimulating"
      technology policies: the state's role was not to replace markets but to make them
      work for learning.
    </p>
    <p>
      He defended the position against formidable opposition. When Paul Krugman famously
      dismissed competitiveness as a "dangerous obsession", Lall replied that for a
      developing country &mdash; permanently constrained by its balance of payments and
      the structure of what it can sell &mdash; competitiveness is neither dangerous nor
      an obsession, but the development problem itself. The argument was conducted, on
      both sides, with respect; there is a pleasing symmetry in the fact that Krugman
      would later hold the visiting chair that bears Lall's name.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="measurement">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px;">
    <span class="eyebrow">Fourth contribution</span>
    <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.6rem,1.2rem+1.6vw,2.2rem);">Measuring competitiveness &mdash; and confronting the hardest cases</h2>
    <p>
      Characteristically, Lall wanted the argument to be settled by measurement rather
      than assertion. He built a <span class="hl">classification of exports by technological
      level</span> &mdash; resource-based, low-, medium-, and high-technology &mdash; that
      allowed the upgrading of a country's trade to be tracked over time, and used it to
      map the manufactured exports of the developing world. The classification is still
      in routine use across trade and development research. Because export structures are
      path-dependent and hard to change, he showed, <span class="hl--coral">what a country sells
      today shapes what it can become tomorrow</span>.
    </p>
    <figure class="bookcover bookcover--left">
      <img src="{{ "/assets/img/books/promoting-industrial-competitiveness.jpg" | url }}" alt="Cover of Promoting Industrial Competitiveness in Developing Countries" width="360" height="485" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>Commonwealth Secretariat, 1999</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The measurement work flowed directly into policy. Several of the indexes deployed
      in UNCTAD's <em>World Investment Report</em> were of his invention; the same
      instincts underpinned benchmarking exercises for the Commonwealth Secretariat
      &mdash; including <em>Promoting Industrial Competitiveness in Developing
      Countries</em> (1999) &mdash; and shaped UNIDO's <em>Industrial Development
      Report</em> and its Competitive Industrial Performance index, which continue to
      rank the world's economies in a frame Lall helped design.
    </p>
    <figure class="bookcover">
      <img src="{{ "/assets/img/books/failing-to-compete.jpg" | url }}" alt="Cover of Failing to Compete" width="360" height="506" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>With Carlo Pietrobelli &middot; Edward Elgar, 2002</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      And he took the framework to the hardest cases. In <em>Failing to Compete</em>
      (2002, with Carlo Pietrobelli), he examined why the technology systems of
      Sub-Saharan Africa &mdash; the institutions that should connect firms, skills,
      finance, and knowledge &mdash; were failing to support industrial learning, and
      what liberalisation without capability-building had cost the region.
      <em>Industrial Competitiveness in Africa</em> (2005, with Erika Kraemer-Mbula, then
      his doctoral student) pressed the East Asian comparison: African economies were
      falling behind not for want of openness, but for want of the
      <span class="hl">skills, technological effort, and FDI strategy</span> that openness
      requires to pay off.
    </p>
    <figure class="bookcover bookcover--left">
      <img src="{{ "/assets/img/books/industrial-competitiveness-africa.jpg" | url }}" alt="Cover of Industrial Competitiveness in Africa" width="322" height="500" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>With Erika Kraemer-Mbula &middot; Practical Action, 2005</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Throughout, the method was the same: patient assembly of firm-level and trade
      evidence, deployed without deference to any school. He went into the field for his
      data because he did not believe these questions could be settled from a desk, and
      he built teams of young researchers to gather it with him &mdash; many of whom went
      on to lead the field themselves. He made, as one tribute put it, no concession to
      the populists on one side or the neoliberals on the other &mdash; a stance that
      earned him devotion from his students and respect from practitioners as well as
      fellow scholars.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--mist" id="legacy">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px;">
    <span class="eyebrow">Legacy</span>
    <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.6rem,1.2rem+1.6vw,2.2rem);">An unfinished argument, vindicated</h2>
    <figure class="bookcover">
      <img src="{{ "/assets/img/books/understanding-fdi-assisted.jpg" | url }}" alt="Cover of Understanding FDI-Assisted Economic Development" width="360" height="547" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>Ed. with Rajneesh Narula &middot; Routledge, 2006</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Lall died on 18 June 2005, at sixty-four, at the height of his powers. His last
      major project appeared the following year: <em>Understanding FDI-Assisted Economic
      Development</em> (2006), edited with Rajneesh Narula, which returned to the question
      he had opened three decades earlier and asked whether the world needed
      <span class="hl">a new agenda for foreign investment</span> &mdash; one that judged FDI by
      the domestic capabilities it helps create rather than the capital it brings.
    </p>
    <figure class="photowide">
      <img src="{{ "/assets/img/sanjaya-garden-party.jpg" | url }}" alt="Sanjaya Lall in black tie, seated on a lawn at a summer party" width="1100" height="737" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>In black tie with family at a summer garden party</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The tributes were immediate and have not stopped &mdash; and it is striking how
      much of them dwells not on the books but on the man: the gentleness and humility,
      the cheerful torrent of e-mails, the open door and the time given, without
      calculation, to young scholars from the countries he studied. He treated his
      students as colleagues, and they repaid him by becoming the field. Colleagues and
      former students produced <em>Evidence-Based Development Economics</em> (2012), a
      volume of essays in his honour whose title distils his method into three words. In
      2011 the Sanjaya Lall Memorial Fund endowed the Visiting Professorship this Centre
      now hosts; the inaugural event drew over eight hundred people to hear Amartya Sen,
      Martin Wolf, and the chair's first holder, Robert Wade. In the years since it has
      become one of the most prestigious visiting appointments in economics, bringing
      Nobel laureates and Clark Medallists to Oxford each Trinity Term &mdash; among them
      Dani Rodrik, Paul Krugman, Esther Duflo, Joseph Stiglitz, and Daron Acemoglu
      &mdash; a good number of whom work on the questions of institutions, industrial
      policy, and technological change that <span class="hl">he did much to open</span>.
    </p>
    <figure class="bookcover bookcover--left">
      <img src="{{ "/assets/img/books/evidence-based-development-economics.jpg" | url }}" alt="Cover of Evidence-Based Development Economics" width="337" height="500" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>Ed. Pietrobelli &amp; Rasiah &middot; University of Malaya Press, 2012</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The clearest vindication, though, is the state of the argument itself. The
      propositions Lall defended when they were heresy &mdash; that technological
      capability is built by deliberate effort; that states can and do shape comparative
      advantage; that openness pays only where the capacity to learn exists &mdash; now
      sit at the centre of economic policy from Washington to Brussels to New Delhi, as
      governments rediscover industrial strategy for the age of semiconductors, green
      technology, and artificial intelligence. <span class="hl--coral">The questions the Centre
      for Technology and Society asks about emerging technologies are, in a direct line
      of descent, his questions</span> &mdash; who builds the capability, who captures the
      gains, and what institutions and policies can widen both.
    </p>
    <p>
      His name also attaches to the journal he helped found. The Board of <em>Oxford
      Development Studies</em> awards the <a href="https://www.devstud.org.uk/2025/06/09/the-sanjaya-lall-prizes-for-2023-and-2024/">Sanjaya Lall Prize</a> each year for
      the best article in that volume, and every second year a larger Sanjaya Lall Student
      Prize for the best article by a student. The recent winners &mdash; on the cost of
      forced solidarity in Côte d'Ivoire, on why women in India fail to reach the schemes
      meant for them, on the developmental legacy of pre-colonial political centralisation
      in Ghana &mdash; are recognisably work in <span class="hl">the tradition he set</span>:
      close, evidence-led, and about how institutions shape what people can actually do.
    </p>
